#: ../../source/docs/common-magnetic-interference.rst:81
#: 4a73f5b8a2db4db598bec03c2a265a3e
msgid "The magnetic field increases as a function of the enclosed loop area Pi R^2"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-magnetic-interference.rst:84
#: 2ff117d4d4ac45ab9e3ebbabc03eb3d2
msgid "Big loop = big magnetic field"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-magnetic-interference.rst:85
#: a9dbf5223fa346c2975b6714dc5f5c67
msgid "Reduce the magnetic field by twisting the loop to close it. aka twist the wires together and keep your return path close to the source"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-magnetic-interference.rst:88
#: f99030287a134a3da1b3bf2f302fc2d9
msgid "In the case where you have a PDB and 4 ESCs you have 4 loops that start at the Deans connector on the PDB, go out to the ESC and return back to the Deans connector.  In the case of the 4 in 1 ESC the current flow is much more concentrated in one area and you don't have the big loops so consequently you decrease the magnetic field."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-magnetic-interference.rst:95
#: 58330158d3a7482aa2921660e301d975
msgid "The magnetic field increases as a function of current"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-magnetic-interference.rst:97
#: cab210d0ab4740c091518c1fda13d13b
msgid "Where there is the option, you can deliver the same power and decrease the magnetic field by increasing the voltage and decreasing the current."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-magnetic-interference.rst:101
#: f0a83ebe093c434c9fe2f57ceec05227
msgid "The magnetic field decreases as a function of the cube of the distance"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-magnetic-interference.rst:104
#: c66306dbf76d42feb63e331fe1e7e88f
msgid "It is more complicated when you are close to the loop but when z >> R the denominator goes to z^3"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-magnetic-interference.rst:107
#: a9f8f2cdbbad4ca1bba80f899d2f0d5b
msgid "There is one more thing and that is that the direction of the magnetic field depends on the direction that the current flows so when you rotate a battery it changes the magnetic field produced by the current flowing in the battery."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-magnetic-interference.rst:112
#: 46d26b8f95054f3a952e489617b1842a
msgid "It can't be stated enough that minimizing the enclosed loop areas and moving the compass away from the current will help things work better and \"yes\", power distribution boards with big circular thick high current PC traces on them produce significant semi-spherical fields as large or larger than their diameter."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-magnetic-interference.rst:119
#: 0e979968ef324cbc9317fad80ec2d0b2
msgid "Natural and Artificial Magnetic Anomalies Warning"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-magnetic-interference.rst:123
#: 719bb8fdf75a458982e147137924ad0d
msgid "The following information has not been objectively tested to determine its impact on a vehicle's compass accuracy in flight."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-magnetic-interference.rst:126
#: 3aaac718d0e840d8ac5338e8df6a5cd1
msgid "Many things can distort the earth's magnetic field in the area you are flying:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-magnetic-interference.rst:129
#: 1b42181b225b4614a44e8ce762538e66
msgid "Steel framed or reinforced concrete buildings, bridges and roadways, iron pipes and culverts, high power electric lines, heavy equipment, trucks and automobiles, steel tanks, electric motors and even computers."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-magnetic-interference.rst:133
#: 21c84933110f499dace429ccaa663e7f
msgid "Flying between steel framed or reinforced high rise buildings will distort the magnetic field in addition to causing GPS multi-pathing."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-magnetic-interference.rst:137
#: fd4f8e353cd0401aa0135763fec10b3f
msgid "Safe distances for compass calibration"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-magnetic-interference.rst:139
#: fe5d4348bdaa4d17987f4bf48694f614
msgid "6\" (15 cm) minimum: Metal rim glasses, pen/pencil, metal watch band, pocket knife, metal zipper/buttons, belt buckle, batteries, binoculars, cell phone, keys, camera, camcorder, survey nails, metal tape measure."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-magnetic-interference.rst:143
#: f684e781751b441da522c13755877fe1
msgid "18\" (50 cm) minimum: Clipboard, data collector, computer, GPS antenna, 2-way radio, hand gun, hatchet, cell phone case with magnetic closure."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-magnetic-interference.rst:146
#: 80232d4f51384a349a1d3ea86d256e56
msgid "6 ft (2 m) minimum: Bicycle, fire hydrant, road signs, sewer cap or drain, steel pole, ATV, guy wire, magnets, chain-link fence, bar-wire fence, data collectors"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-magnetic-interference.rst:150
#: 2eb4beb5e5cb41db8ddc677de6600415
msgid "that use a magnet to hold the stylus."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-magnetic-interference.rst:151
#: 410fbc72f01b4c0eb37db1065621a66c
msgid "15 ft (5 m) minimum: Electrical box, small car/truck, powerline, building with concrete & steel."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-magnetic-interference.rst:153
#: 909b8da2d306403c895eb9b88c660a69
msgid "30 ft (10 m) minimum: Large truck, metal building, heavy machinery."
msgstr ""

